  "user_scenario": "You are Imogen Vasquez-Thorburn, a retired school librarian from Columbus who has shopped at Northgate for years. You speak in full, courteous sentences, a little apologetic about the mess you are in, and you like to understand what you are agreeing to before you agree. On Saturday you set your progressive glasses on the floor beside the sofa and then stepped on them: the frame snapped at the bridge and the left lens cracked right across. It was entirely your own fault, nobody else's, and nothing was wrong with the glasses before that. You open by saying you broke your progressive glasses by stepping on them and you would like Northgate Optical to replace them; you have heard there is a charge for accidental damage and you are prepared to authorize and pay a reasonable replacement charge today. You want the replacement made exactly like the pair you have now, same frame and the same lenses, and you are calling about that pair only.\nYou give your details only as they are asked for. Your phone number is 6145550537. Your date of birth is 1967-11-02. If asked for more, your home ZIP code is 43221 and your email is imogen.vasquezthorburn@fastmail.com. Your membership number is 100537204861 if anyone wants it; it is a regular Club membership you have had since 2013. If asked which glasses, they are the progressives you ordered at the optical counter in February, a black-and-gold Ray-Ban Clubmaster frame; you also have an older pair of reading glasses from 2024, but those are fine and are not what you are calling about. The glasses are for you; you are the patient. If asked how you usually pay, you use your Northgate Visa ending 4417. If asked about the history of the progressives, you remember that you could not get used to the first lenses and they were remade in a different progressive design in March, and that your eye doctor changed your prescription in the summer and they were remade again; you think you paid something like sixty dollars that time, but you do not remember the exact figure and you would rather the representative look at the account than rely on your memory. You do not know any other amounts on the account.\nIf the representative explains that something on your account has to be put right or posted before the replacement charge can be worked out, you say that is fine and that they may go ahead and do it for you; you do not ask for details beyond what they offer. You never ask for anything else to be changed, refunded or sent to you. When the representative tells you the replacement charge and hands you the authorization step, you use it yourself with exactly the eyewear job, the remake number and the amount the representative gave you, and you do not haggle; if the amount sounds higher than you expected you may say so once, but you accept it if the representative confirms it. Once you have authorized it and the representative tells you the replacement has been sent to the lab, you thank them warmly and end the call.",
